# Lessons 10-16
L = []

L.append(dict(id="governance", title="Corporate governance", area="Professional Development",
 pis=["PD:213","PD:214","PD:301","PD:302","PD:303"],
 why="Five governance PIs had 21 exam hits combined, the most of any topic.",
 cards=[
 ("Corporate governance","The system of rules, practices and processes by which a company is directed and controlled for its shareholders and stakeholders.","In short: who makes decisions, how those decisions are checked, and who the decision makers answer to."),
 ("Why governance matters","Minimizes unethical behavior, reduces risk, prevents mismanagement and builds investor confidence.","Poor governance lowers investor confidence and limits profitability."),
 ("Board of directors","Elected by shareholders to govern the corporation. A fiduciary for the shareholders.","Sets overall strategic direction, protects shareholders' investments, hires and oversees the CEO, approves governance policies."),
 ("Independent directors","Board members with no ties to management, who can oversee objectively.",""),
 ("Audit committee","Board committee of independent directors that oversees financial reporting and the auditors.",""),
 ("Code of conduct","Formal written standards of ethical behavior for all employees.","A direct way to build a culture of integrity."),
 ("Bylaws","A corporation's internal rulebook: how it's run, how decisions are made, shareholder rights.",""),
 ("Internal audit","A company's own review of its controls and governance practices.","Ensures governance practices are sound and issues get addressed."),
 ("External audit","Independent CPA firm's review of whether the financial statements are accurate and fairly presented.","Focus: proving the accuracy of the financial statements."),
 ("Internal controls","Processes that make reporting reliable, prevent fraud and keep the company compliant.","Separation of duties is the classic example."),
 ("Transparency and disclosure","Sharing accurate, timely information with shareholders and the public.",""),
 ("Agency problem","Managers (agents) may act in their own interest instead of the owners' (principals).","Governance aligns them through board oversight and pay tied to performance."),
 ("Internal governance mechanisms","Controls inside the company: board, bylaws, internal audit, code of conduct, pay policies.",""),
 ("External governance mechanisms","Controls from outside: government regulations (SEC, SOX), external auditors, stock exchange rules, activist investors.",""),
 ("What changes governance structure","Going public (strict regulation, high transparency), growth, new laws, ownership changes, scandals such as a director charged with fraud.",""),
 ("Stakeholders","Anyone affected by the company: shareholders, employees, customers, suppliers, community, lenders.",""),
 ("Governance and decisions","The board guides decision-making so the company pursues its mission efficiently and ethically. Adds approvals and checks.",""),
 ("Employee's role in governance","Follow the code of conduct, report wrongdoing, keep accurate records, avoid conflicts of interest.","When employees see how their role serves company goals, they feel a greater sense of purpose."),
 ("Whistleblower","An employee who reports wrongdoing. Protected from retaliation under SOX and Dodd-Frank.",""),
 ]))

L.append(dict(id="careers-clients", title="Finance careers, networking, client service, CRM", area="Professional Development",
 pis=["PD:152","PD:153","CR:012","CR:024"],
 why="23 exam hits across these four PIs. Mostly common sense if you know the vocabulary.",
 cards=[
 ("Finance career areas","Banking, accounting, corporate finance, insurance, securities and investments, personal financial planning.","Accounting careers involve financial documents, spreadsheets and bookkeeping."),
 ("Fund (portfolio) manager","Develops investment strategies and oversees portfolios for clients.",""),
 ("Securities and investment careers","Brokers, traders, analysts and investment bankers. High pay, but very stressful with long hours.",""),
 ("Personal finance careers","Financial planners and advisors helping individuals manage money, save and invest toward goals.",""),
 ("Other finance roles","Loan officer, credit analyst, underwriter (judges risk for loans or insurance), actuary (insurance math), claims adjuster, auditor, compliance officer, controller, treasurer, CFO.",""),
 ("Credentials","CPA (accounting), CFA (investment analysis), CFP (financial planning). FINRA licenses: Series 7 and Series 63.",""),
 ("Networking","Building professional relationships that bring information, referrals and opportunities.","The main payoff of a network: information, referrals and opportunities you wouldn't find alone."),
 ("Professional associations","AICPA, CFA Institute, Financial Planning Association, American Bankers Association.","Joining lets you serve on committees, hold offices and attend seminars."),
 ("Conferences and seminars","National conferences build broad contact networks. Hosting finance seminars for small businesses brings new clients.",""),
 ("Referral system","A structured way to get happy clients to recommend you.","A happy client referring friends is the payoff of great service."),
 ("Relationship building","Informal time, like a round of golf with a vendor, helps you get to know contacts.",""),
 ("Finance professionals' client duties","Give appropriate, suitable advice, keep information confidential, avoid or disclose conflicts of interest, provide services clients can't get on their own, understand client expectations.",""),
 ("Suitability","Recommending only products that fit the client's goals, risk tolerance and situation.",""),
 ("Know Your Customer (KYC)","Verifying a client's identity, finances and goals before serving them.",""),
 ("Customer relationship management (CRM)","Strategies, processes, people and technology to build long-term customer value.","Purpose: provide products and services customers want."),
 ("CRM technology","Software that puts all customer data and interactions in a single hub, like Salesforce or HubSpot.","Reveals sales opportunities and customers' prior purchases."),
 ("Human judgment in CRM","CRM collects huge amounts of data, so people must tell useful information from unnecessary information.",""),
 ("Data mining in CRM","Sorting large data sets to find patterns, then analyzing and visualizing the results.","Two prediction types: regression (predicts a number) and classification (predicts a category)."),
 ]))

L.append(dict(id="data", title="Databases, data mining and information", area="Information Management",
 pis=["NF:124","NF:009","NF:014","NF:015","NF:083","NF:149","NF:150","NF:110","NF:001"],
 why="NF:124 (advanced databases) was the single most tested PI: 7 hits in 3 exams.",
 cards=[
 ("Database","Organized collection of data. Field = one data item, record = one complete entry, table = a set of records.",""),
 ("Query","A request for specific data from a database, using criteria.","To find Springdale customers with purchases over $100,000, set criteria on both the address and amount fields."),
 ("Multi-table query, first step","Pinpoint exactly what question you want the database to answer.",""),
 ("Data filtering","Selecting a smaller subset of data for analysis using criteria.","Filter broad to narrow: industry, then country, then analyst rating."),
 ("Normalization","Restructuring a database to reduce redundancy (duplicate data) and improve data integrity.",""),
 ("Primary and foreign keys","Primary key: unique ID for each record. Foreign key: links one table to another.",""),
 ("Relational database","Data stored in related tables linked by keys. Queried with SQL.",""),
 ("Benford's Law","In many naturally occurring data sets, the leading digit is small: 1 leads about 30% of the time, 9 under 5%.","Used to detect fraud. Works only on data that is measured, not assigned (not phone numbers or IDs). Fabricated data often shows digits appearing about equally."),
 ("Information system","System that collects and reports data to support decision-making.",""),
 ("Business records","Official documented evidence of a company's transactions and financial activities.",""),
 ("Internal records","CRM and sales records show customers' prior purchases and preferences.",""),
 ("Environmental scan","Collecting information about outside factors.","Political: laws and regulations. Economic: interest rates, taxes. Social: family roles. Technological."),
 ("Data mining","Finding patterns and relationships in large data sets.","Rule induction finds if-then relationships."),
 ("Data mining ethics","Using people's data without their knowledge, like their search history for targeted ads, raises privacy concerns.",""),
 ("Primary vs secondary data","Primary: collected for your purpose. Secondary: already exists.",""),
 ("Mean, median, mode","Average; middle value; most frequent value.",""),
 ]))

L.append(dict(id="ei", title="Emotional intelligence and ethics at work", area="Emotional Intelligence",
 pis=["EI:001","EI:008","EI:012","EI:019","EI:023","EI:025","EI:092","EI:124","EI:128","EI:134"],
 why="9 questions at regionals. The answers are usually the most professional, mature choice.",
 cards=[
 ("Emotional intelligence","Recognizing and managing your own emotions and others'. Self-awareness, self-regulation, motivation, empathy, social skills.",""),
 ("Assertive vs aggressive vs passive","Assertive: stating needs respectfully. Aggressive: ignoring others' rights to get your way. Passive: not speaking up.","Practice assertiveness by starting conversations."),
 ("Positive attitude steps","Think positively, look good, have a sense of humor, surround yourself with positive people.","Treating a hard new task as a chance to learn = think positively."),
 ("Self-confidence","Belief in your own abilities. Comes partly from accepting that mistakes are part of growth.",""),
 ("Self-control techniques","Know your triggers and avoid them when you can (stimulus control). Pause, breathe or count to ten before reacting.",""),
 ("Tolerance for ambiguity","Being comfortable with uncertainty, such as not needing an immediate answer to every question.",""),
 ("Ethics and ethical dilemma","Ethics: moral principles of right and wrong. Dilemma: a choice between competing values.",""),
 ("Unethical behavior","Using sick leave to play golf, padding expense reports, taking credit for others' work.",""),
 ("Building trust","Keeping promises is the most important way.",""),
 ("Persuading others","Do your homework (have facts), know your audience, show the benefits.",""),
 ("Selling ideas","Convincing others to adopt your idea.","Convincing your parents to give an allowance = selling an idea."),
 ("Herd mentality","Following others without thinking.","Challenging the status quo means thinking for yourself instead of following the crowd."),
 ("Considering conflicting viewpoints","Makes you more empathetic and leads to better decisions.",""),
 ("Constructive feedback","Specific, about behavior, balanced and actionable.","\"Your voice was clear. Add more eye contact.\""),
 ("Motivating employees","Recognition, new challenges or projects, growth opportunities.",""),
 ("Leading change","Change leaders are persuasive, communicate the vision and involve people.",""),
 ("Managing commitments","Break big tasks into smaller steps, track deadlines and only commit to what you can deliver.",""),
 ("Accepting risk","Good decision makers accept risk when the benefit, like reaching an important goal, outweighs it.",""),
 ("Stress at work","Caused by unpleasant environments, overload or conflict. Manage with exercise, time management and support.",""),
 ("Conflict-resolution styles","Avoiding, accommodating, competing, compromising, collaborating (win-win).",""),
 ]))

L.append(dict(id="econ-ops", title="Economics and operations", area="Economics",
 pis=["EC:002","EC:005","EC:006","EC:010","EC:011","EC:016","EC:018","EC:072","OP:024","OP:025","OP:163","OP:519"],
 why="Together these are 12 questions at regionals.",
 cards=[
 ("Goods vs services","Goods are tangible. Services are intangible, can't be stored, vary in quality and are produced and used at the same time.",""),
 ("Industrial supplies","Goods a business constantly buys and uses up in operations, like paper and cleaning supplies.",""),
 ("Law of demand","Price up, quantity demanded down (an inverse relationship).",""),
 ("Price and value","Price shows how much value the consumer places on a good or service.",""),
 ("Factors of production","Land, labor, capital, entrepreneurship.",""),
 ("Profit factors","Raise revenue or cut expenses, like eliminating free services.",""),
 ("Types of business risk","Economic, natural (hazard), human, market.",""),
 ("Protectionism","Government policies (tariffs, quotas) that protect domestic industries from foreign competition, often to protect jobs or shrink a trade deficit.",""),
 ("GDP, inflation, unemployment","Total output; rising prices; people looking for work who can't find it.",""),
 ("Market structures","Pure competition, monopolistic competition, oligopoly, monopoly.",""),
 ("Specialization","Dividing work so each person does one task. Increases productivity.",""),
 ("Law of diminishing returns","Adding more of one input eventually adds less and less output.",""),
 ("Operations","Transforming resources (inputs) into goods and services.",""),
 ("Overhead / operating costs","Day-to-day costs not tied to one product: utilities, rent, insurance.",""),
 ("Employee's role in expense control","Eliminate wasteful use of supplies, be productive, protect assets, follow procedures.",""),
 ("Quality control","Making sure products meet standards and minimizing errors.","Six Sigma: improve quality and reduce inefficiencies."),
 ("Quality culture","Top executives are committed and involved and take responsibility for quality.",""),
 ("Project management first step","Identify the project's stakeholders.",""),
 ("Purchasing","Buying the right goods at the right price, quality and time.","Finding the same quality at a 15% lower price is good purchasing."),
 ("Cash security","Divide cash responsibilities among different employees.",""),
 ]))

L.append(dict(id="comm-pd", title="Communication, customer relations and career skills", area="Communications",
 pis=["CO:014","CO:016","CO:031","CO:039","CO:058","CO:084","CO:092","CO:094","CR:004","CR:010","PD:012","PD:018","PD:126","PD:252"],
 why="About 25 questions at regionals: Communications, Customer Relations and the general Professional Development PIs.",
 cards=[
 ("Lateral communication","Between coworkers at the same level.","Upward goes to your boss. Downward goes from a manager to staff."),
 ("Written communication","Creates a lasting record to reference later, which avoids misunderstandings.",""),
 ("Informational message","Shares facts or data, like explaining a new policy.",""),
 ("Persuasive message","Tries to get the reader to act, like a collection letter to an overdue customer.",""),
 ("Progress report","Simple report that keeps departments updated on activities.",""),
 ("Open vs closed questions","Open questions get full, detailed answers. Closed questions get yes, no or a short fact.",""),
 ("Mirroring","Matching your communication style to your audience to build rapport.",""),
 ("Choosing a channel","Official, non-urgent news for everyone goes in a formal company-wide email. Sensitive news goes face to face.",""),
 ("Phone etiquette","Smile when answering: your attitude comes through in your voice.",""),
 ("Evaluating sources","Check the author's credentials, the date, bias and purpose.",""),
 ("Customer service mindset","Follow up to confirm the customer is satisfied.",""),
 ("Handling complaints","Listen, empathize, offer solutions within company policy, follow up.",""),
 ("Brainstorming","Generating as many ideas as possible without judging. Building on others' ideas is encouraged.",""),
 ("Creativity and flexibility","Flexibility is a core trait of creative people.",""),
 ("Innovation vs invention","Innovation: something new or improved, like automating an assembly line. Invention: something that never existed.",""),
 ("Goal setting","Biggest benefit: a clear sense of direction.",""),
 ("Chain of command","The formal line of authority. Take problems to your direct supervisor first. Going over their head bypasses the chain of command.",""),
 ("Interviewing","Research the company, listen carefully, answer with specific examples, ask your own questions and follow up within 24 hours.",""),
 ("Marketing concept","Satisfy customer needs while making a profit. Social factors like influencers affect buying.",""),
 ("Management functions","Planning, organizing, staffing, directing (leading), controlling.","Controlling monitors the work effort."),
 ("Fair hiring","Use the same hiring process for every applicant.",""),
 ]))
